fishbowl@conservatory.com (james) writes:
> [ Ye Olde Puddinge Farming Debate ]
> Want a fix for all this abuse? Make Rodney wake up at a certain time.

Nethack is broken, because people can cheat by taking all the time
they want to think about what they want to do next. Obviously, your
character wouldn't be able to do this, so it's cheating.

I know how to fix this! Change the game so if you go more than five
seconds without pressing a key, it acts as though you'd typed '.' .
Also, make checking your inventory take time (say, one turn per every
ten items in your pack, rounded up).

That will punish all those nasty cheaters, and thereby make my own
life more fulfilling (through some mystical mechanism I can't quite
articulate).

> Then give the AoY a half-life.

If you make the half-life be 50000 turns, that means there's a
non-zero chance it'd evaporate after you took your first move. That
sure would make the game more fun.

Don't like patience games? Don't play them. Upset because other folks
on your system get high scores by doing something you don't regard as
skill-intensive? Then your problem is not pudding farming, it's that
nethack score is not an effective measure of player skill. IMHO, that
doesn't mean scoring is broken, but rather that your expectations are.

If there is a problem with scoring, fix the scoring, not the game
play. If there is a game-balance problem, make the smallest and
least-invasive change required to fix it. (For example, if P-farming
were really a "let every noob ascend every game" issue, then make
P-splitting respect the extinction limit.)

Creating some sort of a "shot clock" (much less one that's
sufficiently strict to nerf any possibility of farming) turns every
game into a sprint, and breaks lots of styles of play other than the
one you set out to break. There's already a phase of the game that is
a sprint, and it's the part I like the least.


